{"query_id": "rag-eval-001", "query_text": "What is machine learning?", "ground_truth": "Machine learning is a subset of artificial intelligence that enables systems to learn and improve from experience without being explicitly programmed. It uses algorithms to analyze data, identify patterns, and make decisions with minimal human intervention.", "contexts": ["Machine learning is a branch of artificial intelligence (AI) and computer science which focuses on the use of data and algorithms to imitate the way humans learn, gradually improving its accuracy."], "label_source": "human-validated", "metadata": {"annotator": "annotator_1", "category": "machine_learning", "difficulty": "easy"}}
{"query_id": "rag-eval-002", "query_text": "How does FAISS vector search work?", "ground_truth": "FAISS (Facebook AI Similarity Search) is a library for efficient similarity search and clustering of dense vectors. It uses various indexing methods like IVF (Inverted File Index) and HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World) to quickly find nearest neighbors in high-dimensional spaces without exhaustive search.", "contexts": ["FAISS is a library for efficient similarity search and clustering of dense vectors. It contains algorithms that search in sets of vectors of any size, up to ones that possibly do not fit in RAM."], "label_source": "human-validated", "metadata": {"annotator": "annotator_1", "category": "retrieval", "difficulty": "medium"}}
{"query_id": "rag-eval-003", "query_text": "What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)?", "ground_truth": "Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is an AI framework that retrieves relevant documents from an external knowledge source to ground large language model responses, reducing hallucinations and improving factual accuracy by providing relevant context to the LLM before generation.", "contexts": ["Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is the process of optimizing the output of a large language model by referencing an authoritative knowledge base outside of its training data sources before generating a response."], "label_source": "human-validated", "metadata": {"annotator": "annotator_1", "category": "rag", "difficulty": "easy"}}
{"query_id": "rag-eval-004", "query_text": "What is BM25 in information retrieval?", "ground_truth": "BM25 is a ranking function used by search engines to estimate the relevance of documents to a given search query. It is based on the probabilistic retrieval framework and considers term frequency, inverse document frequency, and document length normalization.", "contexts": ["BM25 is a ranking function used by search engines to estimate the relevance of documents to a given search query. It is based on the probabilistic retrieval framework developed in the 1970s and 1980s by Stephen Robertson, Karen Spärck Jones, and others."], "label_source": "human-validated", "metadata": {"annotator": "annotator_1", "category": "retrieval", "difficulty": "medium"}}
{"query_id": "rag-eval-005", "query_text": "What is Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)?", "ground_truth": "Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) is a rank fusion method that combines results from multiple retrieval systems by assigning scores based on the reciprocal of each result's rank. The formula is 1/(k + rank), where k is a constant (typically 60), and scores from multiple systems are summed to produce a fused ranking.", "contexts": ["Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) is a method for combining the results of multiple search engines. It assigns each document a score based on its rank in each individual result list, using the formula 1/(k + rank), where k is a constant typically set to 60."], "label_source": "human-validated", "metadata": {"annotator": "annotator_2", "category": "retrieval", "difficulty": "medium"}}
{"query_id": "rag-eval-006", "query_text": "What is a vector database?", "ground_truth": "A vector database is a type of database that stores data as high-dimensional vectors (embeddings) and enables similarity search by comparing vector representations using distance metrics like cosine similarity or Euclidean distance. It is commonly used for storing embeddings from machine learning models for semantic search.", "contexts": ["A vector database is a type of database that stores data as high-dimensional vectors (embeddings), which are numerical representations of data such as text, images, or audio. Vector databases enable similarity search by comparing vector representations using distance metrics like cosine similarity."], "label_source": "human-validated", "metadata": {"annotator": "annotator_2", "category": "database", "difficulty": "easy"}}
{"query_id": "rag-eval-007", "query_text": "What is cosine similarity?", "ground_truth": "Cosine similarity is a measure of similarity between two non-zero vectors in an inner product space. It measures the cosine of the angle between two vectors, ranging from -1 (completely opposite) to 1 (identical), with 0 indicating orthogonality. It is commonly used in information retrieval to measure document similarity.", "contexts": ["Cosine similarity is a measure of similarity between two non-zero vectors in an inner product space that measures the cosine of the angle between them. Its value ranges from -1 to 1, where 1 means the vectors point in the same direction, 0 means they are orthogonal, and -1 means they point in opposite directions."], "label_source": "human-validated", "metadata": {"annotator": "annotator_2", "category": "mathematics", "difficulty": "easy"}}
{"query_id": "rag-eval-008", "query_text": "What is sentence-transformers?", "ground_truth": "sentence-transformers is a Python framework for state-of-the-art sentence and text embeddings. It provides easy-to-use methods to compute sentence embeddings that can be used for tasks like semantic textual similarity, semantic search, and paragraph embeddings.", "contexts": ["sentence-transformers is a Python framework for state-of-the-art sentence, text and image embeddings. The initial publication was in August 2019. The framework allows you to compute sentence / text embeddings for more than 100 languages."], "label_source": "human-validated", "metadata": {"annotator": "annotator_2", "category": "embeddings", "difficulty": "easy"}}
